# NativeAOT and trimming

The server, the CLI, and apps that embed the client libraries ship as NativeAOT single binaries; development runs on the JIT. Both must behave the same, so shipped code is written for NativeAOT first.

- Every project under `src/` is `IsAotCompatible`. The trim, AOT, and single-file analyzers run in every build, and their warnings are errors.
- No reflection that the trimmer cannot see: `Type.GetType(string)`, `Activator.CreateInstance(Type)`, `MakeGenericType`, `MakeGenericMethod`, `GetMethod`/`GetProperty` by name, `System.Reflection.Emit`, `Expression.Compile`. Code that needs a type-driven table is generated by a Source Generator or written by hand.
- No `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers]`, `[RequiresUnreferencedCode]`, `[RequiresDynamicCode]`, or `[UnconditionalSuppressMessage]` to silence an analyzer. If one seems needed, the design is wrong for AOT; raise it on the Issue.
- JSON uses System.Text.Json source generation (`JsonSerializerContext`) only, never reflection-based serialization. ASP.NET Core uses `WebApplication.CreateSlimBuilder` and the source-generated context for every request and response type.
- Interop uses `LibraryImport` and unmanaged function pointers, not `DllImport` with runtime marshalling.
- Generic virtual methods and generic interfaces instantiated over many value types grow the binary. When one is added on a public type, the PR reports the size of the NativeAOT sample before and after.

## The check

A PR that adds or changes a `src/` project's public surface, a dependency, or interop runs a NativeAOT publish of the executable or sample its Issue names, on the OS the Issue names:

```powershell
$env:PATH += ';C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer'
dotnet publish <project> -c Release -r win-x64 -o <scratch>
```

The PR's `## Verification` states the warning count (zero), the size of the executable, and that the published executable ran its smoke scenario. For `libelasticlm` (a shared library, `src/ElasticLM.Native`), the smoke scenario is `tests/native/run.ps1`: the exports against the header and the C test against the published library, its activation part against the published `elm-service` and its lease part against the published `elm-server` over https (design §17.4, §17.5, §17.6).

One kind of warning is not counted: on macOS, dsymutil's `…/clang/ModuleCache/….pcm: No such file or directory`. The Swift parts of .NET's Apple crypto (CryptoKit, which carries AES-GCM) hold debug references to the clang module cache of the machine that built them, and dsymutil, which writes the debug symbols beside the executable, does not find it on the runner. The smoke step still runs the macOS executable. It first showed when M5b used `AesGcm` (PR #114), and the owner decided to leave it out of the count in CI. The trim, AOT, and compiler warnings stay at zero. Reports of the same warning in other projects: https://forums.swift.org/t/swift-behavior-of-gmodules-and-dsyms/23211 , https://help.codemagic.io/articles/8012313508-why-do-i-see-no-such-file-or-directory-pcm-warnings-when-using-xcode-26-caching-on-codemagic .

A size that grew is split by what added it before the PR says so: publish with `-p:IlcGenerateMapFile=true` and compare the map files.

`HttpClient` in an executable that had none adds about 0.76 MB on win-x64: `System.Net.Http` 420,419 bytes, the name resolution, socket, and primitives parts 61,766 bytes, and their type descriptors 275,180 bytes (M4c3, `elm-server`, PR #79). A design that gives an executable an HTTP client shows this size in its Grill.

Under `dotnet run`, a project with `PublishAot` reports `RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported` as false too. To tell the builds apart at run time, use `System.Runtime.JitInfo.GetCompiledMethodCount() > 0` (JIT).
